From caf-talk Caf Jul  4 00:51:24 1994
From: cps@math.canterbury.ac.nz (Christopher Stephens)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Banned newsgroups at Canterbury
Date: 4 Jul 1994 04:43:30 GMT
Message-ID: <2v841i$og@cantua.canterbury.ac.nz>

Disclaimer: The contents of this article is the opinion of the author
only. Is is not neccessarily the opinion of either the University of
Canterbury, nor the Department of Mathematics. All attempts have been
made to keep this article accurate, however I do not claim there are
not errors.

Last year the University of Canterbury removed a number of newsgroups
that were previously available on the USENET. The policy for removing
newsgroups was something like, remove all news groups with the word
sex or erotica in their title although somehow the following
newsgroups have SURVIVED:
	
        alt.angst.xibo.sex
        alt.politics.sex
        alt.magick.sex
        alt.christnet.sex

I have tried to obtain a list of the banned newsgroups but have not
been able to so far, I shall post again when I have this list. (It is
supposed to be a "matter of public record" but nobody seems to know.)
The list certainly includes:

	alt.sex
	alt.sex.*
	alt.binaries.pictures.erotica*

The stated reasons for the ban were...
1) A proposed change in New Zealand law was about to come into force
and legal advice was given to remove the newsgroups.
2) There was an incident of sexual harrasment by an engineering student
with pornagraphic material obtained on the net.
3) There was *much* media coverage given to "offensive" material on the
net, and the university "relies upon public support"
4) Schools log into the Universities computers and the newsgroups are
obtainable by school children (high school I believe).

The proposed law change *never* happened. The current act, the 
"Indecent materials act 1964", means that any material held
by the UoC is not illegal.
[The current act a) does not cover electronic storage
                 b) it is not illegal to hold indecent materials, only
                    illegal to sell, hire, print or display in a public 
                    place
                    (the definition of public place does not include
                    computer networks)
                 c) lack of malicious intent is a defence.
                 d) much of the material on the banned news groups is
                    not indecent under the Act. ]

The order for the removal of the newsgroups was given by The Director
of the Computer Service Center, Mr Robin Harrington <E-Mail:
R.Harrington@csc@canterbury.ac.nz>. The newsgroups were removed by
stopping the feed from Waikato University, New Zealand. Waikato
continues to recieve the groups. 

No other action to prevent people
obtaining "offensive" material was taken. Although the use of accounts
is restricted by various departments.

Because the ban continues, or the at least the removed newsgroups have
not been reinstated, I have decided to take up the fight for academic
freedom. This means that I am going to present a case for the
reinstatement of the newsgroups. Robin Harrington sound receptive to
this idea and so I am cautously optimistic.

I would apprecitate any pointers to relivent materials, encouragment
or anything else you can send my way. Remember that, this is New
Zealand, so U.S. law and policy does not really apply. However the
University of Canterbury Charter does say:

"The University recognises the widely accepted principles of Acedemic
Freedom..."

Thanks
