>> public entity.
> 
> "Access to" and "ownership of" (or even "control of") are not equivilant.
> Whether Usenet is ACCESSIBLE by some publicly owned or controlled
> computers is not relavent.  Usenet is NOT owned by those same publicly
> owned sites, and therefore is NOT subject to these conditions that
> you wish to see established.

First, I object vehemently to your characterization of any conditions as that
which I "wish to see established."

I can only presume that you have not been following this thread closely.  If
you had, you would know that I have merely been stating my opinions as to
what would would be *legal* under US law.  I have said absolutely *nothing*
as to that which I "wish to see established" and I have *repeatedly*
disclaimed any such wishes.


That said, I understand that there is some confusion as to what I mean, so
I'm going to offer an analogy to try to clear it up.


Suppose that SomeState University creates forum for discussion.  The forum is
housed by a large room.  At one end of the room is a television camera and a
giant projection television.  At the other end, is a crowd of faculty, staff
and students who have come to take part in the discussion.

Now, the tv camera and screen are linked via satellite to Private University,
Private Company, and AnotherState University, each of which has set up a
similar forum.

In the SomeState University forum, faculty, staff and students at SomeState
University hold discussions among themselves and with participants at Private
University, Private Compnay and AnotherState University, via the satellite
link.

What we have at SomeState University is a *local* forum.  Expression coming
into that forum comes from local participants, and from remote participants
via satellite.

The only question is how SomeState University can control the expression that
takes place within *its* local forum.


Now, let's assume that the forum at SomeState University was created as a
limited public forum to facilitate discussion of alfalfa sprouts.  Mitch's
claims notwithstanding, SomeState University does have the power to limit
discussion within the forum to the topic of alfalfa sprouts.

How can it do this?

There are two basic things it can do:

1) It can control local participants.  This can take the form, for example,
of warning them if they wander off-topic, disciplining them for doing so, or
even booting them out of the room entirely for repeatedly disrupting the
discussion of alfalfa sprouts.

2) It can *not* control remote participants; however, it can control the
*access* of remote participants to SomeState University's local forum.  In
other words, if Jow Blow at Private Company keeps talking about bean sprouts,
SomeState University can blank out the satellite signal whenever Joe Blow
appears on the screen talking about bean sprouts.

*None* of this says that SomeState University *should* adopt such
tactics--just that it *may* adopt such tactics, as reasonable measures to
restrict the local limited public forum for its intended purpose.


By now, it should be clear what I mean when I talk about local control over
local access to a local limited public forum in the form of a Usenet
newsgroup.

The local participants are, well, the local participants.  The remote
participants are the remote participants.  The only difference is that
instead of a room with satellite-linked television equipment, we have
computers connected by an internet.

When SomeState University creates local access to some newsgroup,
alt.alfalfa-sprouts, which is restricted to discussion of alfalfa-sprouts, I
would argue that it has created a local limited public forum.  It can
accordingly institute measures to actually enforce topic restrictions in that
forum.  Those measures can include direct actions in dealing with local
participants, but can not include any direct actions in dealing with remote
participants.

SomeState University can prevent Joe Blow's bean sprout posts from being
offered by SomeState University's alt.alfalfa-sprouts news server, but it
cannot prevent it from appearing at Private University, Private Company or
AnotherState University.  SomeState also cannot prevent it from appearing in
SomeState's local feed of alt.bean-sprouts.

In other words, the local control extends only to control over the *local*
forum.  It does not extend to control over any other forums (public or
private) associated with the local forum.


And again, my opinion that SomeState University *can* do this is by no means
a suggestion that SomeState University *should* quash Joe Blow's discussion
of bean sprouts in alt.alfalfa-sprouts.


