Okay, *my* opinion; ignore as you see fit.  :-)

Usenet *as* *a* *whole* is a public forum.  Just as a public library with
meeting rooms cannot screen people out because of their viewpoint or the
words they choose to use, neither can Usenet.

The public library *is* permitted to establish reasonable restrictions on
the use of their meeting rooms.  For example, if other groups have already
collectively scheduled meetings which fill all the meeting rooms at a
certain time, you cannot schedule a meeting at that time; further, there
must be a bona fide meeting (no scheduling to prevent others you don't
approve of from meeting, without the burden of showing up), and excessive
violations of this rule can justify the library's refusal to permit that
group to schedule any more meetings.  And in addition, the library can
put limits on *how* *often* any group can meet in order to make sure that
other groups have opportunity to meet, and can require an estimate of
group size and the use of the smallest available room that is big enough
for the estimated group (rather than the amphitheater for 10 people).  Not
to mention general rules of tidiness and care of the facility, which can
justify exclusion of particular groups *or* particular individuals.

Some of these rationales for restriction do not directly apply to Usenet,
but there are parallels nonetheless.  Instead of meeting rooms we have
newsgroups, and we don't need to worry about a group "meeting" so often
that it in effect excludes other groups.  But there are costs associated
with having a group, so we have a procedure whereby a group (for general
circulation, as opposed to site-specific) doesn't happen unless there is
a reasonable number of people asking for it (a bona fide meeting, in
effect) -- which will tend to result in discovery of any existing groups
that would suffice.  We also reasonably can have rules regarding tidiness
and care of the facility (e.g. limit crossposting, no spamming) which
apply to everyone in all groups.

Remember, all that is Usenet *as* *a* *whole*.

Any specific site -- well, why did the site administrator establish it?

ci.seattle.wa.us is *not* a public forum, it exists specifically for the
use of the City of Seattle in conducting its official business.  That
site is fully justified in excluding any and all groups which aren't 
relevant to its conduct of its official business.  

connected.com was
established as a public forum for paying customers.  Like any facility
it has limited resources and would be justified in restricting newsgroups
to those its customers ask to have access to or which in the opinion of
the management *should* be available (note two separate means by which
inclusion of a group might be justified, no means by which a non-justification
can overrule a justification), subject to the limits of its resources.

700.club.org would not be a public forum.  It might be a *limited* public
forum.  (Note, I have no idea if this site exists.)  Such groups as
alt.pagan would be clearly outside the charter of such a site.

Each of these cases can be shown to be analogous to some specific type
of building-owning organization which includes meeting facilities in its
buildings.

Now for newsgroups.

Just because Usenet is a public forum, does not mean that every group is
also a public forum.  To go back to the public library, the individual
meetings are not public fora and it would be reasonable -- given appropriate
provocation -- to forcibly remove a person from one meeting on the basis
of the subject he is discussing or the opinion he is expressing on that
subject.  It might be equally reasonable to suggest that he transfer
himself to a different meeting in another part of the building.  For
example, if the local Gay Pride organization and the Foursquare Gospel
PAC are simultaneously meeting, a person who loudly proclaims that 
homosexuals should be burned at the stake *should* be removed from the
first meeting and might be welcome at the second.  (Note I don't say he
*would* be welcome -- consult the people participating in the meeting.) 

The large majority of newsgroups are *limited* public fora.  They are for
discussions of certain subjects, sometimes with restrictions on viewpoint.
Evangelical Christian preachers are not welcome to ply their trade in 
alt.pagan, even when they talk about pagan religions.  alt.censorship is
for discussion of censorship from *both* sides, but not for discussion
of the intricacies of Novell network administration.  (Then there is
comp.sys.novell, where the discussion largely is about the intricacies
of Novell network administration.  The beauty of Usenet is that there is
room for another newsgroup -- always.)  In a limited public forum,
freedom of speech exists *only* within the scope of the forum's charter --
even if that charter is unwritten.  

A tiny few -- misc.misc is the only one which comes to mind -- are public
fora.  Anyone here actually read misc.misc regularly?

And there are some *private* fora -- the moderated newsgroups.  Nobody
may speak there without permission of the appropriate moderator.   

Usenet as a whole is threatened by those things which violate the
integrity of Usenet.  Such things obviously include spamming, which can
crash sites.  

But the essence of Usenet is the abundance of limited public fora which operate
under it, so something which creates a sufficiently large violation of the
integrity of the limited public fora also violates the integrity of Usenet.
(Note that I don't try to define "sufficiently large".)  Likewise
anything which allows posting to a moderated newsgroup *without* the
consent of the moderator.  These things justify action by Usenet as a
whole.  (Too bad that "Usenet as a whole" is not capable of action.)
Obviously, it justifies action by any individual site administrator 
*within* usenet, at least in regard to the facilities under that person's
control and *suggestions* to other site administrators.

A site administrator might also decide that while something doesn't affect
the integrity of Usenet as a whole, it threatens the integrity of that
site. For example, if an individual is in the habit of posting messages
sufficiently inflammatory, unpleasant, or damaging to the integrity of
individual newsgroups as to draw several thousand pieces of email
on each of several occasions... warnings are appropriate and if they aren't
effective then termination is appropriate.  IMHO if the violation does not
threaten Usenet as a whole, action beyond the site is not justified -- of
course the person who must decide that question is the same person who must
take the action at the site.

Sorry for rambling on so long, but it's a complicated subject.  
