I would maintain that spamming restrictions are indeed in the public interest.
I use as a precedent the long-standing FCC regulations (which have, I believe
passed Supreme Court tests) regarding "protection of the network."  Oh, one
SC decision is the famous Carterfone decision, which implicitly upholds the
concept of network protection but required the Bell Operating Companies to
provide a method of allowing customer premisis equipment to be connected
to the network through some sort of protection device.

Such a scheme works when you have a single supplier of equipment; in the case
of NetNews you would need to have an overall authority.  The ruling authority
is in the case of the Internet a distributed function -- distributed to the
system administrators.

Spamming (if I understand the definition) causes harm to the network.  There is
no content sensitivitity in the harm caused.  Spamming is not a normal use of
the news facility, and the practice causes the harm directly.


>The restriction must be specifically tailored to further a *significant*
>government interest.  It must *actually* further that interest.  The
>interest *must not* be restricted free speech.  The restriction must make
>*as limited* an abrigment of free expression *as possible*.  And, the
>restriction must be *viewpoint neutral*.



Restriction by subject *is* a viable mechanism for controlling spamming,
because there is a place for just about any subject you want to bring up.
You are confusing content *censorship* with content *categorization*.
Precedent here is in the Courts themselves, which have rules regarding
when comments on specific subject may be brought up, and by the Legislature
which follow Robert's Rules of Order and further have Rules committees to
schedule the topic of events.

Watch C-SPAN sometime when a bill is being debated.  Watch how our Congress-
critters *stick to the subject*.  Watch what happens when one strays from
the topic.

In order for the "interest" to restrict free speech it must act to suppress
that speech.  The subject limitations do not suppress that speech.

Categorization by subject is, in my opinion, the most limited manner of 
controlling harm to the network without suppressing any speech.

Categorization by subject means that the various statements, regardless of
viewpoint, are available to all who can afford them.


