limited public forums.

The only dispute remaining is as to whether viewpoint-neutral subject
restrictions on newsgroups can be proper.  On this point, I suspect we will
never agree.

The essence of your claim that they cannot rests on your contention that such
a restriction *can* represent an improper suppression of speech.  Of course,
this is not the standard that applies.  The court will, generally, find a
rule to be proper if there *exists* a legitimate justification for the
restriction.  The fact that the restriction *could* have been the product of
viewpoint discrimination is not relevant unless it is shown that the rule
*actually is* just a facade for such viewpoint discrimination.

Consequently, the fact that *some* people espouse anti-spamming restrictions
because the object to the content of particular posts does *not* affect the
validity of the claims that I and others have made with respect to what
*could* constitute a legal regulation.

By your argument as to what constitutes a "significant" interest and what
constitutes a "reasonable" restriction, you would have us believe that there
is *never* any significant interest in organizing information and that no
restriction to create such organization is *ever* reasonable.

It follows from your argument that a public library which accepts book
donations must allow the donor to specify how donated materials will be
catalogued and shelved.  It follows from your argument that a government
newspaper that accepts classified ads must allow advertisers to specify the
classification of their ads, without any regard as to whether the
classification bears some relationship to the ad.

By your argument, it is sufficient that individuals feel that dissemination
of their materials will be enhanced by avoiding classifications, to deem the
classification system an improper suppression of expression.

If I donate "One Flew Over The Cuckoo's Nest" and believe that it would be
more widely noticed in the Reference section of the library, then it is
improper suppression of expression for the librarian to catalog the book as
fiction.  If I believe that an ad to sell my car will gain wider
dissemination if placed in the housing or personal sections of the
classifieds, it is an improper suppression of expression for the newspaper to
insist that the ad be carried in the automotive section of the classifieds.

Likewise, if I believe that dissemination of my post on the JFK assassination
conspiracy will be enhanced by cross-posting to comp.sys.mac.apps, it would
*categorically* be improper suppression of expression to prohibit such.

So we now seem to agree on the relevant legal doctrines.  But as I find your
general position to represent an untenable *application* of the law that we
have both cited, I have no expectation that we can come to any agreement as
to the application of law to the situation under discussion.

I'm perfectly willing to agree that occasional misclassified posts represents
no great threat to Usenet, just as an occasional misplaced book represents no
great threat to a library, or an occasional misclassified ad represents no
great threat to a newspaper.  However, I cannot agree that classification and
organization of discussions within Usenet represents an inherent, improper
suppression of expression, any more than I could agree that cataloguing
systems in libraries or classifications in newspapers represent inherent,
improper suppression of expression.


