This statement is so muddled, I am not quite certain what you are saying.

The Supremes have presented clearly and consistently what is required for
a restriction on free speech to be considered "reasonable".  It takes a
whole lot more than a "legitimate" justification.  The existence of a
"significant" state interest is necessary when the limited public forum
is created that imposes such restrictions.  To impose additional restrictions
after the fact, a "compelling" state interest is necessary.  Both
"significant" and "compelling" have very specific legal meanings and are higher
standards than "legitimate".

Whether or not some rule is determined to be the product of viewpoint
discrimination is a matter for the trier of fact to decide (trial judge or 
jury).  One cannot safely predict the outcome of such a decision, in spite
of your assertion, without knowing the specific facts of a case.

Moreover, it can always be asserted.  Once asserted, public statements can be
issued that make the university look like it is actively engaging in
censorship, etc.  All of which would make a university very reticient about
imposing such restrictions in the face of determined advocates of free
expression.  These sorts of battles for human rights are waged partly in
courtrooms, but largely outside of them.


