You keep going on and on ad nauseum with two examples that are wholly
irrelavant to public forum law.  Who ever said that library shelves were
public forums?  Whoever claim the classifieds in your local paper were public
forums?  Talk about setting up a straw man.  The only person I hear making
these suggestions is you and then you proceed to kknock them down as if you
have defeated my arguments.  Hardly.


> Likewise, if I believe that dissemination of my post on the JFK assassination
> conspiracy will be enhanced by cross-posting to comp.sys.mac.apps, it would
> *categorically* be improper suppression of expression to prohibit such.
> 
Could very well be.  If you think you have something to say that is relevant
to both audiences, your restriction would indeed impede free expression.


> So we now seem to agree on the relevant legal doctrines.  But as I find your
> general position to represent an untenable *application* of the law that we
> have both cited, I have no expectation that we can come to any agreement as
> to the application of law to the situation under discussion.
> 
We do not agree on the relevant legal doctrines at all.  You seem to subscribe
to the notion that any plausible restriction meets the legal standard of
reasonable.  Reasonable is not some sort of community standards notion at all.
It has a very clear and consistent definition from the Supremes.  It was even
cited in Perry.  Had you bothered to follow those citations instead of deleting
them from your excerpts, you would have seen precisely what they were referring
to.


> I'm perfectly willing to agree that occasional misclassified posts represents
> no great threat to Usenet, just as an occasional misplaced book represents no
> great threat to a library, or an occasional misclassified ad represents no
