> suppression of expression, any more than I could agree that cataloguing
> systems in libraries or classifications in newspapers represent inherent,
> improper suppression of expression.
> 
In a library, they are vital.  In a public forum, they are not.  Speaker's
corner in Hyde Park Square has gotten along for more than a century now
without assigned soapboxes.

If you are so worried about messages coming unbidden to your favorite Usenet
group then the appropriate response is to form a moderated newsgroup.  Don't
think for one moment that the Supremes would not take judicial notice of the
existence of moderated newsgroups and then tell you if you to start a
moderated newsgroup rather than insist that everyone else surrender their
rights to free expression.  To be reasonable, one of the requirements is
that the restriction make as limited an abridgment of free speech as
necessary.  Moderated newsgroups are therefore more reasonable than
system-wide imposition of censorship.

