Dear Net User:
	As you know, millions of citizens now connect with various 
newsgroups,  bulletin boards, and other nodes on the Internet and 
similar services.  We are political scientists who are interested in 
how network users have begun to use these nodes to discuss social or political 
issues or otherwise engage in civic life.  Your USENET group has been selected 
at random from a list of Internet addresses to post this questionnaire.  
We invite you to fill it out and to return it to us via E-mail.  It  will take 
about 15 minutes.  

	This is an exploratory analysis:  a first attempt to provide a 
general description how citizens are using computer networks to participate in 
civic life.  We are preparing a paper for delivery at the Annual Meeting of the
American Political Science Association in September 1994, and we will be 
pleased to send you the results of our study at that time.

	We will do our best to preserve the confidentiality of 
individual answers.  No names or individual identifications will be 
contained in any report.  As you know, however, the technology of 
the Internet does not allow us to guarantee anonymity.

PLEASE RESPOND WITH "REPLY" .  THIS SHOULD ALLOW YOU TO 
MARK THE APPROPRIATE RESPONSE TO EACH QUESTION WITH AN "X" 
AND RETURN THE COMPLETED QUESTIONNAIRE TO US.  IF YOUR E-
MAIL DOES NOT PERMIT YOU TO MARK THE QUESTIONNAIRE 
DIRECTLY VIA REPLY, PLEASE COPY AND FILL OUT THE 
QUESTIONNAIRE WITH YOUR EDITOR. THEN PLEASE SEND IT TO:
 
        MJMARGOL@UCBEH.SAN.UC.EDU (INTERNET) or
	MJMARGOL@UCBEH (BITNET).

THANK YOU.
Bonnie Fisher, Assistant Professor      Department of Political Science
