From caf-talk Caf Jul  7 18:41:06 1994
From: U15289@UICVM.UIC.EDU
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Cutting through the combustion--a modest proposal
Date: 7 Jul 1994 18:41:04 -0400
Message-ID: <199407072241.SAA21470@eff.org.773620863>

The continuing flame war between the other Mitch and J.S. Greenfield, among
others, seems to me to be losing sight of the most important question WRT
the spamming issue.  What policies might be constitutional, what is a public
forum, etc. are less salient, IMHO, than what is a just and equitable policy
on large-scale crossposting, multiple posting, off-topic posting, etc.

It seems to me that the approach most consistent with the interests of justice
is to declare such postings actionable only when demonstrated to have both an
actual disruptive effect, and an actual disruptive intent.  This would admitt-
edly be heavily dependent on the facts of individual cases, with only limited
guidance from either _a priori_ definitions, or the precedents set by prior
adjudications.

"Disruption," of course, can be viewed from two perspectives.  One is the
interference with the mechanical functioning of a system (_e.g._, mailbombing).
The other, more difficult to deal with, is intentionally making it difficult
to extract the normal and customary information content from a newsgroup,
mailing list, etc.--analogous to shouting down a live speaker.  Up to a point,
readers can deal with this by simply passing over the interfering posts--though
there sometimes can be practical difficulties in accomplishing this.  The best
solution in cases like the rec.pets.cats affair is for readers of good will
simply to stand their ground against organized efforts at invasion/disruption,
and refuse to let the invaders become the dominant force in the group.  Inter-
vention by sysadmins may be warranted from time to time; but let such interven-
tions be driven by actual, demonstrable disruption, not by abstract proscrip-
tions which too often are vague, overbroad, or both.

A few related points:

--Spamming (however defined) may cost some users some extra money; but policies
  should not be driven by this consideration.

--Negative reactions to a post by other users (_e.g._, reactive mailbombing)
  are the sole responsibility of those users.  Should they be disruptive in
  themselves, they should be penalized on that basis.  Admins should not
  penalize a user for the unfounded complaints about one user by others;
  the frivolous lodging of such complaints should constitute an AUP violation
  in its own right.

--Sysadmins should do nothing to enforce newsgroup charters.  If they are to
  be enforced at all, it should be only from within the group, by setting
  the group up on a moderated basis in the first place.

--The good-faith expression of ideas, no matter how _outre_, should never
  be actionable.

