If this is true, then the whole notion of news groups is equally a
resctriction on expression.  But no one seems to be offended by breaking
the news into categories.

   No, they tell the user that there are some audiences who have opted not
   to listen to them.  A different thing entirely.  The right not to
   listen is as important as the right to speak.

Actually, antispamming restrictions are simply regulation of the
appropriate use of the resources.  I imagine there is a practical limit
on the number of newsgroups that can be accomodated in one cross-post,
but the central problem with spamming per se isn't the many off-topic
posts, it's the choice of a post mechanism that avoids the resource and
time saving features of the news readers.

Cantor and Sielgel would have offended me with their post to comp groups
or soc.singles in any case, because they violated the common assumption
of the exclusion of blatant adverstising and ignored the charter topic
statements.  But if the article had been cross-posted, I would have seen
it once and been done with it; it would also have occupied only one
"slot" of space on the spool disk.  The spamming technique of posting it
individually with only those cross-posts needed to cross over to
non-global groups meant that they subverted these mechanisms to force it
to appear in each and every news group I read, and to occupy many times
more space.

The point that's getting lost in all this is that the *content* of the
post isn't the issue.  I think it's clear that C&S could have found
groups in which their post would have been on-topic; if it had been a
post that said, on those groups, "Hi, we do immigration law and we may
be able to give you information that will help with your green-card
application, call or write for details" I don't think *anyone* would
have objected.  Similarly, there exist groups on which advertising isn't
off-topic and in which the whole ad might well be appropriate.  Thus, if
the content of the post could be posted within the conventions, there
can be no issue of content restriction.

The issue about spamming the purposeful contravention of the mechanisms
that have evolved to spare resources.  For this reason, none of the
"freedom of expression" arguments against spamming are in any way
germane.  What is germane is the right of the usenet as a collective to
agree to certain conventions in order to save resources, and to enforce
this agreement among the members of the collective.

