> My point was that if *the Internet* depended upon the benificence of .com
> sites, *the Internet* would not even exist in anything like its present form.

This was true in earlier days of the Internet. It is no longer true, as
the Internet depends VERY MUCH SO on beneficience of COM sites. Especially
now that the majority of sites _are_ COM sites. For example, in the SF Bay
Area, if EDU/GOV stopped carrying Usenet, it would sting a little, but
quickly recover. If COM/NET/GOV stopped, Usenet here would be devastated.

> My point is that if you look at the number of sites that have Usenet feeds,
> in this country you will find that .edu, .gov, and .mil sites outnumber the
> com sites.  I don't care who generates the traffic.  It would not surprise
> me at all to learn that the private enterprise fans are the biggest hogs to
> belly up to a free lunch.

Well, there is no central registry of Usenet feeds. But all evidence
is to the contrary of your stipulation. (I will have to exclude international
"two-letter" domains as it is impossible to easily determine their affiliation,
or get information as to their numbers. But they are dwarfed by US presence.)

Approximately 79% of registered Internet domains are in the COM domain.
The breakdown is as follows:

     COM  79.6%
     ORG   8.0%
     EDU   7.3%
     NET   3.7%
     GOV   1.3%

Most sites will have one or two hosts handling news. Most often one, but 
sometimes as many as a handful (especially with entities that are over wide 
areas, such as multinationals or a small number of GOV sites.)

We can dismiss MIL sites altogether. There are very few MIL sites on the 
Internet, and it is unlikely that any remain as major traffic hubs. Almost all
MIL sites are kept on a separate MILnet, which is _not_ part of the Internet.
Relatively few have the luxury of carrying Usenet.

There are a lot of EDU sites, about a whopping 7%. But there are an 
*ORDER OF MAGNITUDE* more COM sites than EDU. Many of the COM sites carry 
Usenet news (likely, the majority do, as quite a few of these are smaller 
domains created for email/usenet connectivity.) Even if we assume 100%
coverage of EDU sites by Usenet and only 25% of COM, you're assertations 
are wrong. But I think it's less than 100% for EDU and far over 25% for COM.

Every domain that handles news has to have at least one machine with resources 
devoted to Usenet. Even an EDU domain with 1,000 workstation hosts will 
generally have a single news hub, and only a single machine with a presence on 
Usenet. But a COM site with only six machines may also have a single machine
with a presence on Usenet.

You contend that EDU/GOV/MIL presence on Usenet outnumbers COM/NET/ORG. 
But this flies in the face of 79% COM registrations, and the fact that the
majority of posters using Usenet post from COM/NET/ORG (which you seem to
have conceded, as it is easily proven.) What evidence do you have for your
assertations, other than anecdotal?

> I don't care who generates the traffic.  It would not surprise
> me at all to learn that the private enterprise fans are the biggest hogs to
> belly up to a free lunch. 

What are you talking about? Private sites pay real money to be on networks.
Users at government sites are getting the free lunch, spending our tax money.
You seem to be one of them.

> The only important point to this entire line of reasoning was that .edu,
> gov, and .mil sites have more than enough upstream sites among themselves
> to choose from, should a few .com sites decided to play guardian of the
> public morality and try to impose Usenet restrictions.

It is unlikely that any formal restrictions will ever be imposed.
However, should, say, an EDU site make a great pest of itself, I have no 
doubt that the network as a whole would react (again, informally) to either
remove it from Usenet, or mitigate the damages by cancellation or advisory 
deletion. That's how things work, and will continue to work. 

