The issue of limiting students' access to certain sites on the web
is on many people's mind these days.

Although I think that college students should have unlimited access to 
the web, I can understand the practical reasons that teachers at 
elementary and junior high schools might want to make sure that children 
cannot use the school's computer lab to download porn GIFS.

The solutions proposed so far seem to focus on a filter which will stop
students from accessing certain "dangerous" IP sites.  This type of filter
would require that the lab's administrator keep track of the many
questionable sites and regularly update a list of places that are off
limits.  

For many reasons, it seems that this approach is doomed to failure. 
First, a student could access the controversial materials through a
mirrored site.  Secondly, the lab administrator would never be able to 
keep up with the many different questionable sites.  Thirdly, it means 
that thousands of computer lab administrators around the world are going 
to be forced to make pretty subjective value judgements about what sites 
are suitable.

An alternative approach might be to encourage people who administer
potentially "controversial" sites to voluntarily set up a filter on their
end that would block access from any IP address that is identifiable as an
elementary school or junior high school.  This is sort of analogous to
asking owners of a liquor store to place their porno magazines out of
the reach of minors. 

I think that most web administrators would agree to some sort of voluntary 
system like this.  

