Goodness, that just illustrates the cultural differences that have to be
catered for on an international forum.  By what stretch of imagination
are porno magazines sold in liquor stores?  Here, one would not think
of going to a wine merchant or off-licence (to use British terminology)
to buy porno magazines.
 
Your hoped-for standard of "questionable sites" or "questionable
newsgroups" is never going to work.  In an ideal world, they would not
exist because there would be no desire for them.  Down here in the
real world, offensive material can be found everywhere.  I personally
find the use of obscenities (I mean as expletives, not simply as literal
references to e.g bodily functions) in spoken and written language very
offensive.  But I have learned not to get upset by them, and simply to
disregard postings in which I find them used.  I suppose I could create
a kill-file containing all known obscenities, but I'd rather not.
I also find the depiction of violence offensive, especially when it
seems to be done for its own sake rather than to draw a useful lesson:
I would prefer not to have minors exposed to it.
 
No matter how strong your own opinions may be on the
subject of protecting minors, nor how well-founded your concerns,
you are never going to solve them by these ad hoc solutions, and it
is arguable that by attempting any kind of censorship you are exposing
yourself to more serious charges when that censorship fails.
Our charges will eventually have to find their way in this real
and imperfect world: it would be so much better if one could teach
them to cope with the world as they will find it, rather than to
keep them cloistered in ignorance and then throw then out into the
maelstrom.
 
