First, I am arguing that companies like Netcom, who charge explicitly for
access, are common carriers.  You had my argument wrong from top to bottom.
I suspect that comes from your preoccupation with ad hominem.

It is not the charging that makes them common carriers.  It is the nature
of the service they provide.  Netcom is analogous to a telephone company.
It provides the connection between your computer and the Internet.  That
should not entitle them in any way to censor your messages, any more than
the phone company (you need to use them to connect to Netcom, after all).

If you think I am flatly wrong to consider the network service providers
as common carriers, you better tell that to their lawyers, because that is
precisely the stand that many of these companies and many BBS operators are
taking.  CompuServe just took that position in court in the last few days.
They do this in order to avoid being accessories to unlawful behavior of
their clients.  Courts are wisely telling them that they cannot have it
both ways.  If they censor, they are responsible for the contents of every
message.  If they do not censor, they are not responsible.  They can
credibly claim ignorance.


