     The choice was made long ago: Usenet is not now and never has
been an "anything goes" kind of place.  Some corners of "alt" (which
isn't Usenet anyhow) approach this "anything goes" policy.  "alt" was
founded as an "alternative" to Usenet and its formal procedures for
group creation.  However an interesting thing has happened in "alt"...
many alt groups have adopted charters and rules of netiquette that are
as strict as those common in Usenet groups.  These groups have decided
that a little politeness and order isn't such a bad thing after all.

     The charters of Usenet groups and Netiquette in general are
intended to enhance rational discourse rather than free expression
(there are a few exceptions, as is only proper).  There needs to be
a balance between anarchy and order in a newsgroup to permit the conduct
of rational discourse.  Allow too much anarchy and the result is the
"let a thousand monkeys post" syndrome with the readers looking for
Shakespeare.  Eventually that newsgroup loses its most vital posters.
Impose too much order and the result is a sterile newsgroup with few if
any posters or readers.  Get the balance right and you have a vibrant
newsgroup with a high diversity of viewpoints being expressed and
tolerated.

